 animal I'm hunting. If that's an elk, that's more than a deer. Yeah, for sure. A deer kill kit's about that big or even smaller. An elk kill kit might be a little bigger. So we're talking like football size for a really good elk kill kit where you've got four game bags for your four quarters, another fairly decent size game bag for your back straps, tenderloins, neck meat, any internal organs you're going to take, whether it's heart, liver, and whatnot, tongue. Yeah. Some of those are great eating, you know, and they have so much nutrition. If, by the way, if you're ever out of food and eating a fresh kill in the back country, focus on the heart, the liver, the tongue, things like that, because you will get a lot more vitamins and nutrition. Three to five times more out of an organ than you do out of pure meat. Most people don't realize that. And eat some fat. Yeah. Because that gives you, you know, real fuel. Yeah. In the kill kit, the game bags, if you're carrying a replaceable blade knife, first, shame on you. I've seen so many, like, near catastrophic injuries. I've experienced two of them myself with replaceable blade knives. They're scalpel sharp and they're tiny and they're fumbly. They're not easy to handle. But anyway, if you're carrying one, take some spare blades in your kill kit. And I do like a pair of rubber gloves, preferably two pairs, so you can hand one to your buddy, so it'll help you. This is especially important if you're bear hunting, because bears can carry trichinella, trichinosis, whatever it is. Yeah. And you don't want to get blood in a cut or blood on your hands and then lick your hands afterward, no matter how good the blood is. Trichinosis. Okay. Let's see. What else? I'm going to have to start referencing my list soon. Here's one. How about a power source to recharge whatever devices you're using? In your phone, yeah, it's kind of important, but funny enough, it's important not for entertainment. It's important for photography. You ne