 little kit assembled by Leupold that I'm looking at right now. But it's actually a fix-it sticks kit with all the items necessary to reset your scope, to torque the screws on your rifle's action, whatever. Tighten screws on your bipod or tripod if they're coming loose. Ammo carrier. I don't leave without one of these. This is, again, on your belts, not a backpack type thing. But I like having ammo on my person, not in my pack. If I have to dig for it, I've probably lost the shot opportunity or the follow-up shot opportunity. We're going to talk a little bit about rifle selection here in a bit and so forth. But for now, one last thing that can become crucial when you never expected it. And that is a little set of lightweight bikini-type crampons, right? These just go over your boots, almost any type of boots. I've used them on muck boots. I've used them on tennis shoes, like trainer types. But the best thing, of course, is a traditional hunting boot. You can put those on and you can suddenly walk with confidence across rain-wet logs to cross creeks. You can go up and down rocky slopes that are wet and slippery, ice. They're also super helpful if you have an injury like I did. Because then you just have that little bit more of reassurance that you're going to be steady walking. Your feet won't go out from under you. I don't have a pair here, but trekking poles are another really good add-on for that. Yeah, for sure. One other thing I want to tell you before we move to the next question. If you wear glasses, by all means, take a spare pair into the backcountry. Yeah. I have lost glasses. I know a story of a guy who lost his coming through oak brush. And I'm functional without glasses. Everything's just a little, the edges are all blurry. But this chap was not functional without glasses. He spent four and a half hours feeling around in the oak leaves trying to find his glasses before friends found him. Because he literally couldn't travel without glasses. And then his 