t stomach sick, some Imodium, it'll slow down diarrhea, can be life-saving. If you get some bad water and you can't stay hydrated in the backcountry, you're in trouble. Yeah. So some Imodium to stop diarrhea, really important. A little bag of Tums. I put some things in my pocket that I tend to carry while I'm hunting out there. I was just going to show you here. A little roll of Tums or Rolaids, right? Keep that with you. Some kind of lip gloss or balm, whatever you want to do, whether you're a little girl or a boy, with some SPF sun protect in it because generally your lips will start getting real dry and cracked. And then I take some kind of a powdered energy supplement. I generally keep at least two with me because when we start packing meat, I want my buddy to be in a good mood, too. So I give him one and I'll mix one in my drink. I like choosing stuff that has electrolytes and other crucial things in it as well as some caffeine to help you perform. Mm-hmm. That's the fundamentals of my frequently used emergency essentials, right? Then if you're worried about somebody impaling themselves on one of their broadheads or a compound fracture where bone comes through the skin or, heaven forbid, a gunshot wound, really the most frequent is a bad, bad cut. Yeah. While you're dressing or boning off or quartering off your animal. Yeah. It can't hurt to have some gauze with a blood clotting agent in it. That's really good. A roll of medical tape. I like Leuko Tape, L-E-U-K-O, the best because it's so durable. You can bind a sole to a boot that's coming off and it'll hold for a couple days with this stuff. It's incredible. You can put a patch on a blister and it'll stay for the duration of your hunt. Leuko medical tape is awesome stuff. Yeah. Splints, I don't worry about because I can take a leg off of a bipod or tripod and use some clothing and some paracord, whatever, and create a splint. I can even do a traction splint on a broken femur or something like that. Yeah. That's about it for my medical kit. That and an emergency plan like that in reach. For sure. Yeah. Now, folks, back when I was an outfitter and a professional big game guide, my brother and I required our guides to have wilderness first responder certification. There was two levels. You could do a two-day wilderness first aid course or you could do a 10-day first responder course. Man, they teach you everything from evacuation methods to, like I mentioned, how to put a traction splint on a broken femur, how to diagnose pregnancy issues. They didn't, thank heaven, teach us how to deliver babies. Yeah. But if you're planning, yeah, if you're planning to spend a lot of time in the backcountry, it really behooves you to at least take something like that two-day wilderness first aid course. Because you'll learn with the equipment you have with you, even if it's quite a modest assortment, you can handle most injuries. You can create pressure bandages. You can create splints. And you can create crutches and carry slings to get partners out and so forth. Yeah. So I think that's about all I got for you on the emergency essentials. Okay. When it comes to picking a scope for your hunting rifle, making a good choice can make or break your hunt. So choose something with premium quality glass so that it provides clarity, contrast, and color purity. Pick a scope that's durable and waterproof. And for hunting open country here in the West or anywhere else around the world, opt for a scope with modern features. A good dial-up turret with a zero stop and ideally a zero lock is most important. Don't go overboard on magnification, on bulk, or on weight. Too much magnification makes you slow to acquire the animal in your crosshairs and prevents you from spotting your own impacts and making fast follow-up shots. Too much bulk is awkward to carry. And too much weight makes your rifle top-heavy and ruins its balance. Not to mention that it's a pain to pack in rough country. I like a scope that