by hooked on these. Oh, really? He saw me walking through terrain that he and our camera guy had to very, very gingerly navigate because it was dangerous. Yeah. And I was just confidently walking through, and then I'd turn around and watch them pick their way through. Oh. Yeah, we had a good laugh over, but he became a convert. Mm-hmm. Trekking poles is another one. Yeah. Sorry, that's not just one item in several. I like it. On the other hand, what's something that you've carried that isn't worth the wait? Oh, gosh. As gear-loving hunters, we tend to always carry more than we need, right? And there are so many items that you can leave behind. To this day, I tend to carry more than I should. Mm-hmm. I think that can be a personal thing because each of us has these inflated ideas of what we may or may not need. Mm-hmm. And the exercise that I've found works best for me to figure out what to eliminate is to do some two- or three-hour morning or evening hunts without a pack at all. So, you'll say, all right, it's warm enough. I don't need my coat, but I'm going to want to eat something. I'm going to put a sandwich in the cargo pocket of one, you know, side of my pants. I've got a bipod in the other one. Vinyl with my tag in it and my belt, pouch with ammunition. Here's my rifle. Off I go. Mm-hmm. For a half day of hunting, that's literally all you need. And you can usually stretch that out to a full day as long as you don't get exposure-related stuff, too much cold, right? Mm-hmm. You can go a full day of hunting on a sandwich, right? Yeah. Probably the thing that I see people pack too much of on a regular basis is water. The trouble with that is you can't risk not having the water unless you're carrying a live straw and there's lots of groundwater around. Because dehydration is a much bigger risk than carrying an extra four pounds of water. For sure. Right. So, you're better off to be safe that way. I confess I don't have a good answer for you on that one. Yes, I do. I just thought of it. Sorry. Too many layers. Light bulb. Yeah. Yeah, that's what I was going to say. We talked about that. Too many layers. People carry their whole entire wardrobe with them. Yeah. Minimize. Especially, like, being a girl. We want to pack all our clothes. But, yeah, definitely clothing is fun. Yeah. Cool. Okay. So, we're going to switch gears a little bit. We've talked about survival, but now we're going to talk about actually killing an animal. So, which gear becomes indispensable, particularly optics, a tripod? A rod, rangefinder, binos, field processing equipment, your rifle. Bullets. Yeah. Good one. Okay. All right. Yeah, I'm glad you went there because, in reality, you know, you can walk off, like I just mentioned, without a pack and without food even. And you can go shoot an animal and have a successful hunt. Yeah. If you have the, basically, the tools to close the deal at the moment of truth. So, hand me that rifle over by you. I've got a pair of rifles here. Both of them are very, very good rifles. One of them is the Browning X-Bolt II Pro that I've been hunting with quite a bit lately. Audrey, you shot your Himalayan tar with this very rifle in New Zealand. It's about a $2,000 rifle, right? It's got a good carbon fiber stock. It's accurate. It's configurable. You've got adjustable cheek rest and so forth. Skeletonized action. Fit with a very nice suppressor. This is an outstanding hunting rifle for a price that won't break the bank. Now, yeah, I was a college student once, too, where a firearm over $500 was simply out of reach. But if you're getting serious about backcountry hunting and you're on a quest to eventually end up with a really good set of equipment, a premium rifle is going to be part of that journey, right? So I didn't bring any of the basic rifles out here that I could have, like a Mossberg Patriot or a Ruger American. Base model Tika is a really good way to get started. But eventually, carbon fiber and Cerakote are going to be your friends in the backcountry. I have had rifles rust to the point where they were almost no longer functional in solo backcountry hunting situations with a lot of grizzly bears around. You don't have a partner with a backup rifle, right? If your rifle goes down because it's not Cerakoted or stainless, you are quite literally screwed, right? You're dead. Sorry. If a bear, yeah, comes at you. So I like the features that give you reliability. Carbon fiber, Cerakote, and a quality build. This rifle is one I highly recommend. It's basically the stripped-down base model of their carbon fiber stocked mountain hunting series. It's lightweight, doesn't have fancy camel paint. It's just black Cerakote. But man, it shoots. Yeah. And it performs. It's very reliable. We can talk about scope here in just a minute. I want to just briefly mention this rifle now. We have not hunted with this one. It's new to us. It was built in Canada by Alpine Riflecraft. Really good carbon fiber stock that they make. All titanium action with a controlled feed bolt. It's got that big Mauser-type clog extractor, three-position wing-type safety. And this rifle, complete with the scope and the suppressor, is 6 pounds, 15 ounces. It's incredibly light. I don't know if you've run this one. You can't usually accomplish this with a suppressor and a very capable scope on it. We've got two of my favorite scopes here showcasing them. One is the VX5 HD Leupold, right? This is the new Gen 2. Really, really good scope. And that one is a Swarovski. That's specifically the Z5 Plus. They're both basically a 3 to 15, 3 to 18-type power range, which is just perfect. These are both 7mm caliber rifles. This one's a 7PRC. This one is a Wildcat called a 7mm SAW, S-A-W, that's really cool. It's a compact short action that's optimized for long-range use. We'll leave it at that. So, rifle. Good scope. You asked what's essential for hunting, for making the kill at the moment of truth. First, a good rifle. Second, a good scope. Because if you can't shoot your rifle accurately in the backcountry, you're kind of out of luck. And people say, oh, I'd never shoot 450 yards. That's so unethical, blah, blah, blah. It's becoming more and more acceptable now. But the fact is, in many backcountry settings, especially for elk or big alpine country mule deer, sometimes caribou, whatever in Alaska, you may hunt for a full week to 10 days to get one shot opportunity. And when it comes, it could be at a quarter mile. And if you're not prepared to take that shot... You might not get another chance. You might not get it. You probably won't get another chance. So, pairing your rifle with a really good scope is important. This is my go-to. Leupold VX5 HD in a 3-15. Plenty of magnification. It weighs 18 ounces, as is, which is the lightest of its type that I've found with all the features it gives. It has a dual rotation elevation turret, so you can dial up 40 minutes of angle. That's plenty to get you way further out than you need to. I put a good quality level bubble on it because I've seen people hit poorly because they can't the rifle. Audrey's pointing it herself. Yeah, you made a shot that we had. It turned into a rodeo because you canted the rifle. Yeah. Yeah, you forgot to check your bubble, which was my fault. You didn't have one on your rifle and you used mine. And anyway, that's a story for a different time. It's a whole story. Yeah. So, once you've got a really good scope on a really good rifle, mounted in good quality, lightweight mounts. These are Leupold Backcountry mounts, right? It's one-piece base and ring. Then you need a few good quality accessories. We'll add that level bubble into an accessory level. Funny enough, a suppressor now has joined that list. For me, for a while, it was kind of a luxury. Then it became something that I really, really liked and kind of didn't want to go without. Now, here, hold that for me. I just almost refuse to hunt without one unless it's a dangerous game where you need to maximize the compact nature of your rifle, crawling through alders in Alaska or whatever. These are two really good suppressors. Both of them are under 10 ounces. This one's quieter. This one's more compact. This is a Dead Air Nomad TIXC, titanium cross country. And this one is made by Mack Brothers. It's the Zenith K 30 caliber. It's four and a half inches. It's tiny. And on this little compact short action 7mm, it's absolutely perfect. So, that's another thing. Really good suppressor. Can you hand me that bipod down there? This is probably the last piece of the puzzle. This one's got the orange legs. It's a Spartan Precision Equipment Javelin Pro Hunt Tack. I think this one's got lots of names because it's got lots of... Fancy. Yeah. So, it's got the adjustable legs, climbing rubber feet. You pop those off and it's got carbide spikes underneath so you can shoot on ice or granite or whatever and you won't slide. Lots of really cool elements to this and they weigh less than half of a pound. I like the orange legs. You can get them from Spartan Precision Equipment. I like the orange because you don't lose them as easily. That is one thing of a detachable bipod, right, that you just plug in and go with. You can drop that or you can misplace it. But I carry that in my left side pocket and I can quick draw that sucker, plug it in and be prone in a matter of about six seconds to take a shot. Sometimes less if the terrain's favorable. It makes a huge difference in some of those longer shot opportunities and windows of opportunity to close very quickly. Yeah. Next, I mentioned the ammo pouch. I don't go anywhere when I'm hunting without one of these because I can have like four rounds in my rifle, another 10 on my belt, and I'm pretty well set. We don't have time here, but I could share horror stories of hunters that just went with the three rounds, four rounds in their rifle and wounded an animal. And then they had to go hike out two or three hours to get more ammo while that thing lays there and suffers. Yeah. It's not fair to the animal. So those things. Of course, your binos. That's not really a pack item, but it's part of the whole package, right? I carry my knife either on my belt or on my vinyl harness, and I will carry a backup in my hunting pack. So when my buddy's cheap knife or replaceable blade knife goes south, I can hand him a nice knife and he can help me finish cutting up my animal. Yes. Okay, you ready? Yeah. Move on. What's next? Okay, how do you decide where to spend money on gear? So in other words, which components deserve premium quality because failure in the field can have serious consequences? Yes. My word. I know, it's a loaded question. You hit me with all the good stuff. Okay, let's divide this up like we divided the packs in the beginning. If you're doing overnight or let's say two-night trips, you can accept a little less premium quality. But folks, if you're going into the backcountry in, say, Alaska or New Zealand or something like that for a week to 10 days, you cannot risk your gear filling on you. Certain items. So I guess that's what we're talking about here. I'm going to leave the boots. That's kind of an obvious one and it isn't directly related to your backpack. But we'll just give them an honorable mention. Without good boots, you ain't traveling in the backcountry so your hunt's shot. It's done, right? If you're going to be carrying all this gear on your back, you better have a good pack. So I'll say, number one, good backpack. It doesn't have to be the most expensive one in the world, but a good one. I think the least expensive one I would trust a really serious hunt on is that browning pack I mentioned. They're about $400. This pack is between $700 and $800. It's a lot more. Still, if you're dedicating a week, week and a half of your life to this effort and you've planned for it all year and so forth, this item isn't going to... I mean, you're not going to wear that out. No. That's going to last you years. So I'd buy a good backpack. Next, have a tent that's going to hold up. It doesn't have to be a ghastly expensive tent. Usually, you spend money on a tent to reduce weight, not increase quality. So you can have a $250 or $300 tent that's high enough quality. You're just going to have to hump an extra four or five pounds up the mountain. If you can afford it, spend the money to get a lightweight tent. But if you can't, well, I guess cowboy up. Yeah. Right? I would say more than your tent, spend money on a good sleeping bag. And again, if money is tight, buy a high-end synthetic bag. It'll be half the cost of a high-end down bag. You'll have to carry two or three pounds more weight. But it'll stand by you in wet weather. Yeah. So that's where I'd go next. For me then, there's so many interwoven elements. Yeah. Good glass. It's not really part of your backpacking setup, but it's certainly part of your hunting setup. We've transitioned from emergency essentials to hunting essentials. Yeah. A really good binocular, preferably with an onboard rangefinder, will help you find game. If you can't find game, you can't hunt game. Okay? So that's another really important one. And then your rifle. You know, you can get into something for about $1,200 that's going to serve you really well. Audrey's hunting with a version of this Browning X-Bolt II with an injection-molded stock. It's their Speed SPR. And it costs literally $800 less than this one. Very accurate. Very capable. It works just fine. It's got the Cerakote, so it's not going to go south on you. And a carbon fiber stock's probably going to be a little more rigid, a little more forgiving shooting positions and so forth. But, yeah, get a rifle that you can count on. Because at the moment of truth, if it won't go off or it won't put your bullet where you need it to, what's the use? You know? Which brings us to a funny side point. I'm going to segue here a little bit. A lot of people think that accuracy is the most important element of a lightweight hunting rifle. It's certainly hard to achieve. You know, the lighter the rifle is, the harder it is to shoot it accurately. The harder it is for the builder to make it accurate. To me, the number one characteristic is that it goes off every time you try and fire it. It's got to feed, fire, and extract reliably. Because no matter how accurate it is, if it won't go off when you press the trigger, again, what's the use? Yeah. So those two, they go hand in hand. Reliability first, and then accuracy should be insisted on. This is an off-the-shelf production rifle. And with factory ammo and with hand loads, it's a legitimate half-ammo away gun. You know, a friend of mine borrowed this. Yeah. Shot a feral goat, invasive species, right, in New Zealand, at 880 yards with his first shot. That feral goat is probably a 40-pound animal. They're not big. Really good shot. Yeah. Yeah. Shout out to my friend Brian Lipscomb in Montana. He was the accurate man who pulled the trigger on that one. I think that pretty well covers—no, let's talk about ammunition. In backcountry hunting settings, you might be places in remote upstate New York or the Adrindacks—did I say that right? In, you know, the East, something like that where you backpack in and hunt white-tailed deer, maybe black bear. But in most backcountry hunting settings, whether it's New Zealand where you've got animals up to big red stags, or Alaska, or the Rocky Mountain West, you probably are going to have multiple different species. You may not have tags for all of them, but at some point in your life, you hope to hunt them all. It behooves you to pick a cartridge that will work on anything from pronghorn antelope to elk, moose, or whatever. Most of us aren't going to get to shoot very many moose in our lives. Maybe if you live in Alaska, that's different, but a really versatile cartridge that's enough for moose, even if it's barely, and isn't, like, extremely ridiculous for antelope and white-tailed deer and whatnot, is perfect. Combine that cartridge with a good bullet, something that won't grenade on impact like a varmint bullet, something that's going to be accurate, so you can place your shots well at extended ranges. There are a lot of different types, but I tend to like bonded core bullets, where the lead is soldered or chemically or otherwise married to the copper jacket, or monometal bullets. There are some exceptions, like this is the load you shoot in your browning. This is a 6.8 Western with 175 grain, it's really a Sierra-tipped Game King bullet that they've built just for, Browning's ammunition. Good high BC, plenty of sectional density, and it tends to hold together better than most cup and quartile bullets, and I've seen these very capably kill everything from small-bodied deer right up through big zebra stallions, which are known for toughness. So, just to hit a few high points, 6.8 Western is a good versatile cartridge. 2.8 Ackley Improved is my personal favorite. There are others that are as good, but I just love that cartridge. Very, very versatile. 7 mm PRC. Probably the most practical modern cartridge choice there is out there right now. Ammunition's everywhere. Rifles are readily available. It performs very well. It's easy to hand load. Just a good, solid cartridge. I don't think the .30s are necessary, but if you're a good old .30 caliber loving American, great. .30 out of 6 will do the job just fine. .300 Win Mag, .300 PRC. I actually, I'm going to probably hurt some feelings here, but I don't love the .308 for backcountry styles of hunting, because it doesn't handle the really high VC or the really heavy for caliber bullets in that .30 caliber family well enough for what I want. You know, if you're shooting inside 300 yards, fine. .305 for a new hunting rifle. When you're in the market for a new hunting rifle and need one quickly, it can be hard to find a premium precision rifle that's readily available, especially if you've evolved as a rifleman to the point where you know exactly what you want. A light carbon fiber stock, for example, paired with a premium custom level action and a short fast twist barrel optimized for, let's say, shooting modern high BC bullets with a suppressor. And as a discerning rifleman, you're not willing to settle on anything but the best, something that fits your ticket exactly right. What do you do? Well, if you got the time, obviously the best is to order exactly the rifle you want from one of the premium makers, whether it's a high-end Browning or Weatherby or a Fierce or Seekins Precision Rifle or possibly a top-shelf custom job by Gunworks or Proof Research, Park West Arms or the like. But what if you're in a time crunch? Whether your existing rifle went belly up or you've lucked into a specialized wild sheep or grizzly hunt and you need a more powerful cartridge or an ultralight mountain rifle or whatnot, well, you need a new rifle and you need it right away. There are ways. You can search Gunbroker. You can browse the in-stock tabs on custom and semi-custom makers' websites in the hopes that they have something that fits your needs and so forth. There's one other resource that is worth using and that's Porter's Pond, a company that specializes in precision hunting rifles. Shane Porter, the proprietor there, has become a friend of mine and he's not your average small gun shop owner. Shane is a man with impeccable taste in shooting instruments and a penchant for the finer things in life. Porter's shop is currently well-stocked in fine rifles, including some outstanding models by Browning, Proof Research, and Fierce Firearms. To see listings, go to portersfirearms.com or call 870-701-0504. You can also find many of Porter's listings on gunbroker.com. It's a small shop, a specialty shop, owned by a friend and a fellow hunter that's passionate about the backcountry. Check them out. Okay, last question. If you suddenly, so you had all this in your pack, you know, you're good to go, you're ready, but you could add an extra five pounds. What would you put in there? Okay. Snickers bars? Yep. I've got a few things laying out here that I might pick up and add. The first one is going to be, funny enough, an ultra-lightweight shooting bag that's just big enough to serve as a pillow. This one's made here locally by the Day Mountain Ranch that's not too far from our home country. And it's, you know, lightweight fill is expensive. So these are about 80 bucks, but it weighs half a pound, I think. Like, yeah, it's like, it's super light. Like, it's just like. And I use these for multiple reasons. Obviously, it works well as a shooting bag. You can set it across the top of a rock or log and rest your rifle on it. You're shooting with a bipod, use it underneath the back of your stock. So now you've got two points of contact. Super easy to make precision shots that way. But I use it as a pillow. I put it in the hood of my mummy bag, my sleeping bag, and use that as a pillow. And it helps me sleep so much better. And then I also will sit on it. I'll put it on jagged rocks or whatever if I'm hunting somewhere where there's not a comfortable seat for my old half-century old hindquarters. That's really, really nice. The next thing I would do, I would probably add a compact tripod. This one is a Spartan Hoplite. And it's a very versatile tool because you can use it as a shooting rest. It's got the same spigot that plugs into your bipod adapter. And I put an adapter on the bottom of my compact-filled camera. It's a compact digital SLR, so I can run a camera on this. Whether I'm filming a kill shot or setting up to take solo pictures on the timer for myself or doing a time-lapse, whatever, I can do that off of this tripod. So it's a shooting rest. It's a camera tripod. The third and really important feature that I use it for or use that I use it for is a spotting scope. I'll put a little compact but high, high-end spotting scope in my pack as well. And if you're hunting moose, not such a big thing. Even caribou, probably not such a big thing to have a spotting scope. But if you're hunting sheep and you need to count rings or maybe you're hunting chamois or Himalayan tar in New Zealand and you really want to be able to evaluate whether or not they're mature, mule deer, big mule deer across the canyon can be hard to evaluate. And it's great for that. And then, of course, I take it one step further and I'll do some digiscoping. Put my phone camera on that compact spotter and I can zoom it in and get good video of live animals a mile away or a kill shot 500 yards away. And so it's a really useful tool that way. On top of that, I really probably am going to add food. And I laugh and say Snickers bars, but probably going to be that. You know, if you're really trying to save weight, for example, maybe you're flying in a Super Cub in Alaska and you've been given a 50-pound maximum weight for a 10-day trip, that's where you really learn what's important to you and what you can do without. Because you have to weigh stuff and trim and weigh stuff and trim until you've got it down within weight. And usually you end up without any nice stuff to eat. You're going to have Mountain House, one meal, one bag per meal. And you're going to have maybe two extra bags, two, three extra bags. Because when you're flying in, you never know if you're going to get picked up on schedule. The weather dictates that. Always take an extra day or two worth of food. If you make a kill, that's great. Take some salt and pepper too. Seriously. You can eat meat for a week, but if you don't have some seasonings for it, it is gnarly. Yeah, it's hard. And I don't recommend using gunpowder for seasonings. Okay. They say the old timers used to use black powder. Really? Yeah, it tasted salty to them when they got desperate. So, yeah, I would probably take some high-performance luxury stuff, Snickers bar. Yeah. Something like that. Cool. Yeah. Yeah. Well, that's all my questions for you. Yeah, thank you. I have to throw them back at you. Mm-hmm. Now that you've seen all this gadgetry, what are you inspired to upgrade in your personal equipment? Oh. Hmm. Well, I kind of just use a lot of your stuff. But... Don't tell me you're going to upgrade fathers. No. No. Probably my sleeping bag is going to be one for me because I do tend to get really cold. Yeah. And so, I have a good sleeping bag right now, but even maybe just a little bit more, you know, go down to that zero degree weather or something like that. Yeah. That it will keep me warm through anything. Solid plan. Mm-hmm. Yeah. Cool. Well, folks, whether you're watching or listening, I have one other thing to offer you here. I have a gear list that I keep on my computer that's designated my Alaska, New Zealand, DIY gear list. And right at the top, huh, tag and license. Check that out. But it goes through all the different things that you probably should have, including something we didn't talk about because it's really not your backpacking element. But, you know, a backup sidearm perhaps for use in grizzly country or whatnot. But it goes through all the different things that I carry and it has little checkmark type boxes next to it so that you can make sure you've got it in your pack. I'm happy to send this to anybody. Send an email to backcountryhuntingpodcast at gmail.com or better yet, info at globalhuntingadventure.com. And I'll just email you this file. You can print it off and use it yourself. There's a lot on there that we didn't cover, such as, I should mention this, a Leatherman tool, right? I carry this because I can pull thorns out with the jaws of the pliers. I can use the little scissors to trim my mustache. I can file a broken nail with a file or I can use the little saw on it to cut branches. Better yet, you know, if you think you're going to be splitting moose briskets and building miniature log cabins for 